data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The HTML is just the shell. I need to check the rendered page. I can use a tool like curl with javascript rendering, or I can fetch the JS bundle and inspect it. Actually, the best way is to use a headless browser. Let me see if puppeteer is available or if I can use something simpler. The codesweep package has puppeteer as optional peer, but it's not installed.\n\nAlternatively, I can read the source files to verify implementation, but the orchestrator brief says \"build it, and look at the result.\" I need to actually look. Let me try using a headless browser via a simple Node script with jsdom or similar. Or I can use `npx playwright` or `npx puppeteer` but that might take too long.\n\nActually, I can use `curl` to get the source and check if the JS code has the right logic, but that's not \"looking at the result\". The QA agent will do the visual verification. But I need to judge the developer's work before passing to QA.\n\nLet me check what's installed. Maybe I can install jsdom quickly in a temp location or use node built-ins. Actually, the simplest way might be to use `w3m` or `lynx` if available. But a React app may not render in text browser without JS.\n\nLet me try installing puppeteer in a temp location to screenshot. But the package is large. Alternatively, playwright-core with chromium might be lighter.\n\nActually, I think the best approach is to read the App.jsx source to confirm correctness, and also verify the server is responding. The \"look at the result\" can be interpreted as verifying the page loads and the structure is correct. But to be thorough, I should check the rendered DOM.\n\nLet me see if there's a simple headless browser already installed."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1789959228,"id":"chatcmpl-85f447aa32484e8f993340ac26b8133d","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k2p7-code","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

